"Genesis 1:26-28": {
  tag:"identity", icon:"👑", title:"The Tselem — Image-Bearing as Royal Vocation",
  plain:"When God said 'let us make man in our image,' He was installing humanity as His royal representatives on earth. Not just spiritually similar to God — actually deputized to represent His reign.",
  body:"Genesis 1:26-28 is the founding charter of human identity and vocation. 'Then God said, 'Let us make man in our image (tselem), after our likeness (demuth). And let them have dominion over the fish of the sea and over the birds of the heavens and over the livestock and over all the earth...'\n\nThe word tselem (צֶלֶם) in the ancient Near East did not primarily mean a spiritual resemblance. It was a royal term. When a great king wanted his authority to be present in a territory he could not personally occupy, he would erect a tselem — a statue, an image — of himself in that territory. The image represented the king's presence and authority in that location. Every person who saw it knew: this territory is under that king's domain.\n\nGod uses this precise political vocabulary to describe humanity's purpose. Humanity is not merely spiritually similar to God. Humanity is God's tselem — His royal image, installed in the earth to represent His reign, to administer His creation under His authority. 'Have dominion' is royal language — the same word (radah) used for a king's rule. Humans are steward-kings of creation under the sovereignty of God.\n\nThis is why the Nephilim usurpation of Genesis 6 is so serious — they created beings outside the tselem that attempted to fill the tselem role by force. And this is why the Incarnation is so astonishing — God Himself becomes the perfect tselem in human form. Colossians 1:15: 'He is the image (eikon, the Greek equivalent of tselem) of the invisible God.' Jesus is what humanity was always meant to be. He is the true tselem, and the invitation of the gospel is to be conformed to his image (Romans 8:29) — to have the tselem restored.",
  sources:"Genesis 1:26-28; Colossians 1:15; Romans 8:29; ANE royal image theology; John Walton, 'The Lost World of Genesis One'",
  relatedVerses:["Colossians 1:15","Romans 8:29","Psalm 8:5-6","Genesis 5:1-3"],
  thread:"identity", threadOrder:1
},

"Psalm 82:6": {
  tag:"identity", icon:"👑", title:"'You Are Gods' — What Jesus Meant and What David Meant",
  plain:"Psalm 82:6 says 'I said, you are gods' — and Jesus quotes it in John 10:34. Understanding what this means for human identity is one of the most important passages in the Bible.",
  body:"Psalm 82:6: 'I said, 'You are gods, sons of the Most High, all of you; nevertheless, like men you shall die, and fall like any prince.'\n\nThis Psalm describes God presiding over a divine council, judging the divine beings ('elohim, v.1) who were assigned to govern the nations (Deuteronomy 32:8). He condemns them for injustice — for failing to protect the weak and poor. Verse 6 is God speaking to them: 'I said you are gods...' — meaning, I gave you that status and authority — 'but you will die like men, fall like any prince.' Their failure to administer justice according to God's standard means they will lose their divine status and die.\n\nJesus quotes this exact verse in John 10:34-36 when the religious leaders accuse him of blasphemy for calling himself the Son of God. His argument: if the Scripture calls these divine council beings 'gods' — those to whom the word of God came in a delegated authority role — and Scripture cannot be broken, then why do you accuse me of blasphemy for saying 'I am the Son of God,' when I am the one the Father actually sent and authorized?\n\nThe deeper point behind Jesus' use of the Psalm: the human-divine vocation that Psalm 82 describes the divine council failing at is exactly the vocation Adam failed at, and exactly the vocation Jesus fulfills. 'Gods' in the biblical sense is not about nature — it's about function and authorization. Humanity was made to bear God's image, administer God's creation, and represent God's authority on earth. That is a 'godlike' role. It was stripped through failure. It is restored through Christ. Redeemed humanity, in union with Christ, is being restored to the royal image-bearing vocation of Genesis 1.",
  sources:"Psalm 82:1-8; John 10:34-36; Deuteronomy 32:8; Michael Heiser, 'The Unseen Realm'",
  relatedVerses:["John 10:34","Deuteronomy 32:8","Genesis 1:26","Revelation 5:10"],
  thread:"identity", threadOrder:2
},

"Revelation 5:9-10": {
  tag:"identity", icon:"👑", title:"Kings and Priests from Every Nation — The Redeemed Identity",
  plain:"The redeemed in Revelation 5 are described as kings and priests from every tribe, tongue, people, and nation — not a single ethnic tradition, but every human people-group represented before God's throne.",
  body:"Revelation 5:9-10: 'And they sang a new song, saying, 'Worthy are you to take the scroll and to open its seals, for you were slain, and by your blood you ransomed people for God from every tribe and language and people and nation, and you have made them a kingdom and priests to our God, and they shall reign on the earth.'\n\nFour categories: every tribe (phylē — ethnic descent group), every language (glōssa — linguistic community), every people (laos — cultural community), every nation (ethnos — geopolitical group). The breadth is total and deliberate. Not one tradition. Not one culture's reading of the faith. Every human community that has ever existed is represented in the ransomed assembly around God's throne.\n\nThe identity declared over them is royal-priestly: 'a kingdom and priests.' This is Exodus 19:6 language — God's original description of Israel's calling ('a kingdom of priests and a holy nation') — now extended to the entire redeemed humanity. The tselem-bearing vocation of Genesis 1, the royal priesthood of Exodus 19, is restored in full diversity across every people group.\n\nThis vision stands as the final answer to every attempt to claim that any one ethnic tradition owns the biblical narrative. The story was written by Afro-Asiatic Semitic peoples. It was preserved by African, Asian, and Middle Eastern communities before European Christianity existed. It was carried by enslaved African believers in the Americas who found in it a deeper truth than their oppressors intended. And in Revelation 5, every one of those communities — every tribe, tongue, people, and nation — stands redeemed before the throne. The diversity of humanity is not erased in the end. It is gathered.",
  sources:"Revelation 5:9-10; Exodus 19:6; 1 Peter 2:9; Galatians 3:28",
  relatedVerses:["Revelation 7:9","Exodus 19:6","1 Peter 2:9","Galatians 3:28"],
  thread:"identity", threadOrder:3
}

